WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.079
[Submit subtitle corrections at criblate.com]
In January 2024, Google DeepMind released an AI model called Alpha Geometry,

00:00:05.079 --> 00:00:10.558
which could solve geometry problems from the International Mathematical Olympiad,

00:00:10.558 --> 00:00:11.360
or the IMO.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.120
The IMO is the highest level of competitive math contests at the high school level.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.446
Every year, more than 100 countries send six teenagers

00:00:19.446 --> 00:00:21.420
to represent them at the competition.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.715
Each country has its own elaborate system of contests

00:00:24.715 --> 00:00:26.920
leading to their choice of six representatives.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.606
When Alpha Geometry was tested on a database of 30 geometry problems,

00:00:31.606 --> 00:00:33.100
it solved 25 of them.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.840
This is better than the performance of a silver medalist.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.340
This quite understandably generated a lot of buzz.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.080
But here's what nobody talks about.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.430
The most surprising part for me is not that AI managed to solve these problems,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.080
but it's what happened even before the AI showed up.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:54.350
A 25-year-old technique with no AI at all, just using logic and pure equation solving,

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.580
was already able to solve 18 out of 25 problems.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.540
That's already a bronze medal at the IMO.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.320
And then the model hit a wall.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:06.360
There were problems that even this ingenious logical model wasn't able to solve.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.380
And that's where AI comes in.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.367
But instead of leaving everything to AI, the DeepMind team very

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:16.055
cleverly integrated the logical model with an AI component in

00:01:16.055 --> 00:01:19.980
order to reach their spectacular result of 25 out of 30 problems.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.620
So in this video, yes, I do want to talk about AI and geometry.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.648
But before that, I also want to talk about how a non-AI model

00:01:27.648 --> 00:01:31.280
was already better at geometry than the vast majority of humans.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.120
So with that, let's begin.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:34.920
Our task today is simple.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.840
Build a bot that can solve IMO geometry problems.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.000
But I'm going to make a caveat to make our problem a little bit harder.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.800
Write a bot that solves IMO problems with no AI.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.280
To begin attacking this, we're going to use a well-kept secret about geometry problems.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.340
A few key facts can get you very far.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.440
For example, here are two important facts from geometry.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.100
One, when two lines cross, the opposite angles are equal.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.692
And two, if these two horizontal lines are parallel,

00:02:03.692 --> 00:02:08.840
and you look at the Z over here in blue, the angles on the inside of the Z are equal.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:12.260
With these two facts alone, we can already prove a non-trivial theorem.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:16.280
In this diagram, the two orange angles alpha are equal.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:23.060
If you apply our two facts one after another, it proves our theorem.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.260
Now this is how far we could get starting with two facts.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.580
Now imagine how far we could get if we started off with 10 facts,

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.100
or 50 facts, or 100 facts.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.440
The space of theorems we could prove would get larger and larger.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.415
In October 2000, the researchers Tu, Gao, and

00:02:39.415 --> 00:02:43.080
Zhang released a paper that did precisely this.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.200
In their paper, they made a database of 75 geometry rules.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.983
Applying these facts in various combinations, the

00:02:49.983 --> 00:02:52.780
researchers could solve some very hard geometry problems.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.380
These rules start out simple, like the ones I've written on screen.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.632
But as you go on, the rules get more complicated,

00:02:59.632 --> 00:03:01.520
like the rule at the bottom of the screen.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.680
But how do you feed all these diagrams into a computer?

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.421
Well, the naive way to do it would be to plot

00:03:07.421 --> 00:03:09.740
everything on Desmos using explicit equations.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.340
But I think we can both agree that that's far too clunky.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.660
Another alternative is using a language that's specifically designed for theorem proving.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.400
The predominant language in this regard is called Lean.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.224
While Lean is really great for things like algebra and inequalities,

00:03:23.224 --> 00:03:26.240
at the moment it's not very well suited for Euclidean geometry.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.222
To get around this, the authors used a more niche

00:03:29.222 --> 00:03:31.580
language that's specifically designed for geometry.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.372
Now, to the best of my knowledge, this language doesn't yet

00:03:34.372 --> 00:03:37.400
have an official name and is only described in research papers.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:38.940
Here's what it looks like.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.608
Say we want to represent this problem over here to a computer,

00:03:42.608 --> 00:03:46.140
which says that a triangle with two equal sides has two equal angles.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.620
In the specialized machine language, you'd write it like this.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.900
ABC points, which means that there are three points ABC.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.880
AB equals AC, which means that the sides AB and AC have the same length.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.308
Goal, angle ABC, equals angle BCA, which means that

00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:08.100
the goal is to show that these two angles are equal.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.760
The researchers called this method deductive database, or DD for short.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:17.789
The word database suggests that you're starting with a database of key facts,

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:22.180
and deductive means that you're deducing new theorems from those key facts.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:23.520
So how did it perform?

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.280
Well, out of 30 geometry IMO problems, it solved seven of them.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.620
Now, there are two ways to look at this.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.500
On one hand, seven out of 30 isn't very much.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.820
It's not even an honorable mention at the IMO.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.392
On the other hand, the fact that brute force can solve

00:04:39.392 --> 00:04:42.420
even one of the IMO problems is incredibly impressive.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:44.420
IMO problems are very hard.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.140
So how do we bring the score up?

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.209
On one hand, we could just throw our hands in the air and say,

00:04:49.209 --> 00:04:50.700
okay, we need AI to make it better.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.780
But that's not quite true, because DD has a fundamental

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.980
limitation that we haven't addressed yet.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.820
DD can't solve equations.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:00.400
This is a problem.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.980
To see why, I'm going to tell you about a theorem called Thelz's theorem.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.420
The angle in a semicircle here is always a right angle.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.900
Here's a short proof using equations.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.640
Draw a third line connecting the third point to the center.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.705
Then the three lines I'm marking over here are all radii of the circle,

00:05:17.705 --> 00:05:19.160
so they have the same length.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.360
Therefore, the two smaller triangles are both isosceles.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.360
Their opposite angles are equal.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.838
Since the angles of the big triangle ABC sum to 180 degrees,

00:05:29.838 --> 00:05:33.420
we have 2 alpha plus 2 beta equals 180 degrees.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.680
Dividing both sides by 2, alpha plus beta equals 90 degrees.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.880
But alpha plus beta is the angle ABC, so we're done the theorem.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.416
Solving linear equations is something that computers can do very well

00:05:46.416 --> 00:05:49.888
just using linear algebra, so the DeepMind researchers implemented

00:05:49.888 --> 00:05:53.620
this in their model and called it algebraic reasoning, or AR for short.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.180
So to recap, we have 2 modules so far.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.860
Deductive database, or DD, is a hardcoded list of geometric rules.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.780
Algebraic reasoning, or AR, is the ability to solve systems of linear equations.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.180
The DeepMind team used these 2 models alternatingly.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:11.640
So they first ran DD until it stopped.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:16.520
And then they ran AR until that stopped, and then DD and then AR.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.440
And they kept doing this until either the problem was solved or the time ran out.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.540
Together, they called this procedure DD plus AR.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.380
This works really well.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.217
For example, given this problem below from their paper,

00:06:30.217 --> 00:06:32.920
the DD module deduced the first two lines.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:38.080
The AR module then took the resulting system of equations and solved them.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.880
Now here are the results on the IMO geometry problems.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.120
DD alone solved 7 out of 30 problems.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.800
DD plus AR solved 14 out of 30 problems.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.706
The DeepMind researchers pushed it even further to 18 out of 30

00:06:52.706 --> 00:06:56.100
problems by adding in what they call human-coded heuristics.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.840
This is nearly a bronze medal at the IMO.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.780
To me, it's really surprising that DD plus AR does so well.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.839
Because IMO geometry problems are really really hard,

00:07:05.839 --> 00:07:10.220
so the fact that a brute force approach can get you this far at all is very surprising.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.826
And given that we've gotten this far with no AI at all,

00:07:13.826 --> 00:07:17.160
it's worth asking why bother adding AI in the very first place?

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.620
Because our model has a fundamental weakness.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.760
It struggles to make so-called auxiliary constructions.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.163
To solve many hard geometry problems, you have to draw in

00:07:28.163 --> 00:07:31.260
extra lines or shapes that aren't in the diagram already.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:34.010
For example, suppose you want to show that the

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:36.400
sum of the angles in a triangle is 180 degrees.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.060
Here's a very slick way to do it.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.980
Draw two parallel lines at the top and the bottom of the triangle.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.200
This is the auxiliary construction.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.420
The angles labeled alpha on the inside of the Z are equal.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:51.180
Likewise for the angles over here, labeled beta.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:53.840
Now call the remaining angle of the triangle gamma.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:55.620
And now you just solve equations.

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:59.698
We know that alpha plus gamma plus beta is 180 degrees

00:07:59.698 --> 00:08:02.700
because the top purple line is a straight line.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:08.140
But alpha, beta, gamma are also the angles of the triangle, so they sum to 180 degrees.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:09.080
We're now done.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.440
Now the key step was to draw the two lines at the top and bottom.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:16.260
Once we had that, we could deduce the theorem from facts that we already knew.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.120
In a sense, this is what makes geometry problems so fun.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.893
The true gems in the field always feel like adding

00:08:21.893 --> 00:08:24.320
in the right idea seemingly came out of left field.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.349
In IMO geometry problems, you typically need to add not just one,

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:31.140
but several auxiliary constructions to the diagram.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.997
Which is bad news for machines because it means that

00:08:33.997 --> 00:08:36.080
there's an infinite search space at every step.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.340
This is where AI comes in.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.612
The DeepMind team built a language model whose one

00:08:41.612 --> 00:08:45.080
and only job was to produce auxiliary constructions.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:48.098
The input to the language model was the problem statement

00:08:48.098 --> 00:08:50.600
and the steps in the proof that had been produced so far.

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.875
Now I'm writing it in English, but keep in mind that it has to be

00:08:53.875 --> 00:08:56.880
inputted using the geometry coding language that we talked about before.

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:01.040
The output of the language model would be on auxiliary construction.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.040
So an extra point or figure on the diagram.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.960
So given this, here's how Alpha Geometry works in total.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:11.540
First, input the problem statement into the language model.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.120
It will return an auxiliary construction.

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:17.460
Second, give this new diagram to DD plus AR.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.580
It will output a bunch of steps until it terminates.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.200
Feed this output back into the language model.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:25.940
It will produce yet another auxiliary construction.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.080
Feed this new output back into DD plus AR.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:33.280
Repeat this procedure until either the problem is solved or the time runs out.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.742
Put simply, the language model is the creative

00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:39.680
brain thinking of clever auxiliary constructions.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.797
On the other hand, DD plus AR is the logical brain,

00:09:42.797 --> 00:09:45.800
using pure logic to deduce new facts from known ones.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.654
So the creative brain thinks of a clever idea

00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:51.080
and the logical brain deduces things from that.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.996
The creative brain thinks of yet another idea and

00:09:53.996 --> 00:09:56.680
the logical brain deduces even more things from that.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:58.320
And so on and so forth.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:02.012
So we've been talking about this language model and that's all fine and good,

00:10:02.012 --> 00:10:03.813
but if there's one thing we haven't discussed,

00:10:03.813 --> 00:10:05.500
where are they getting their training data?

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:10.780
Because there aren't that many IMO geometry problems with solutions available online.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.240
So you need to do something else.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.800
The Alpha Geometry team circumvented this issue by doing something quite interesting.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:19.500
They generated their own training data.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:23.440
Here's how you can generate a sample problem and solution for your training data.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.640
Randomly plot a bunch of points and lines on the plane.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.580
Then using DD plus AR, deduce everything you can.

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:32.700
Like various angles are equal.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:34.360
Various lines are parallel.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.133
It'll conclude, for example, that the sum of the

00:10:37.133 --> 00:10:39.320
three angles in the triangle is 180 degrees.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.120
Now go backwards.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.360
Erase some portions of the diagram.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:45.700
Now we can state a problem.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:49.760
Given this triangle showed that the sum of the angles is 180 degrees.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:53.926
To solve it, we would need to draw back the lines that we'd erased,

00:10:53.926 --> 00:10:56.360
but those would be auxiliary constructions.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:59.860
So just like that, Alpha Geometry generated some new training data.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.140
They randomly plotted a bunch of lines and points on the plane.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.280
They used DD plus AR to deduce a bunch of known theorems from that.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:09.820
And then they erased some portions of the diagram.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.885
So that if you want to arrive at that theorem yourself,

00:11:12.885 --> 00:11:16.800
you would need to draw those portions back in using an auxiliary construction.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.900
In fact, they generated hundreds of millions of synthetic proof examples.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:25.600
A total of 9 million of them required at least one auxiliary construction.

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:30.040
Here are some examples of the very complicated diagrams that this process produced.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.289
The most complex synthetic proof had an impressive

00:11:34.289 --> 00:11:38.100
length of 247 steps with two auxiliary constructions.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.980
With all this data, they were able to train their language model.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.040
So how did this whole model perform?

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.680
Well, recall that DD solved 7 out of 30 problems.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:50.220
DD plus AR solved 14 out of 30 problems.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:54.740
DD plus AR plus human quadratureistics solved 18 out of 30.

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:59.839
If you combine DD plus AR with the language model with some fine tuning,

00:11:59.839 --> 00:12:02.760
it was able to solve 25 out of 30 problems.

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:05.000
So why is this so exciting?

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.740
Well, to me, Alpha Geometry isn't just about solving triangles.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.958
It's really about something much deeper, about machines

00:12:11.958 --> 00:12:14.520
being able to think and reason like human beings.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.960
Alpha geometry can think creatively, but also very logically.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.000
This is exciting because this general strategy,

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.000
using creativity and logic together, is by no means specific to geometry.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:29.160
It's used in problem solving in any kind of domain.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.660
For example, science, medicine, engineering, you name it.

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:37.253
Of course, alpha geometry wasn't built to solve problems in those other domains,

00:12:37.253 --> 00:12:41.520
but it can give us a glimpse into how machines might try to tackle those problems.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.060
And that's a future I'm really excited for.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:45.380
That's all for this video.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:46.520
Thanks so much for watching.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:47.140
Bye.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:52.180
This video was the second in a series of guest videos that I'm doing this summer.

