He does a great job taking various higher level math topics and then

00:13:01.418 --> 00:13:04.880
distilling them into a bite-sized but nevertheless very substantive summary.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:09.213
Even though alpha geometry was only announced last year, January of 2024,

00:13:09.213 --> 00:13:13.910
already there have been some pretty meaningful updates to the space of AI's solving math

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:14.860
contest problems.

00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:18.023
This year, there were multiple different groups that achieved a gold level

00:13:18.023 --> 00:13:21.100
performance not just on the geometry problems, but on all the problems.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.784
And at least those from Google DeepMind and OpenAI were purportedly

00:13:24.784 --> 00:13:28.207
just doing it with natural language, so no domain specific languages

00:13:28.207 --> 00:13:31.580
like this geometry one, or even a math specific language like Lean.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.006
I was particularly excited when he pitched the idea of doing alpha

00:13:35.006 --> 00:13:37.738
geometry as his guest video because I thought it might pair

00:13:37.738 --> 00:13:40.880
particularly well with a different video that I've got in the works.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.222
It's probably not going to come out until the end

00:13:43.222 --> 00:13:45.660
of the year about the role of AI in math research.

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:49.390
And as part of that project, I'm collecting interviews and stories with various

